129-### Project observability preset family
130-
131-`summary() { observability-project }` returns the whole-project view. The
132-element presets work with the existing `get` and `list` operations; the task
133-preset also applies to Bugs because Tasks and Bugs share the leaf estimate
134-contract.
135-
136-Every preset exposes the same nested `observability` field:
137-
138-- `scope` identifies the project or selected Epic/Story/Task/Bug.
139-- `summary` carries the existing `byType`, `active`, and `blocked` summary,
140-  scoped to that element and its descendants.
141-- `readiness.estimateKind` carries the leaf or calculated
142-  Story/Epic/project projection state without dumping every missing leaf.
143-- `readiness.coverage` always reports `estimated`, `unestimated`, `total`,
144-  `ratio`, and `complete`, including fully estimated scopes.
145-- `readiness.rolledEstimate` is the known Fibonacci sum even when coverage is
146-  partial. Query the separate `estimate` field on the incomplete Story/Epic to
147-  retrieve its exact `unestimatedTaskIds` drill-down list.
148-- `isBlocked` / `blockedElementIds` aggregate hard dependency state across the
149-  scope, while `isAdvisoryBlocked` / `advisoryBlockedElementIds` aggregate
150-  derived cross-container waiting. The nested summary retains blocker details.
151-- Only the project preset adds `primary_goal`. Its active form is
152-  `{active:true,id,revision,objective,updated_at}`; after a clear or before
153-  any creation it is `{active:false,latest_ref}` (where `latest_ref` can be
154-  `null`). Epic, Story, Task, and Bug observability deliberately do not repeat
155-  this board-wide parent intent or treat it as element acceptance.
156-
157-Plain `summary()` keeps its legacy response shape for compatibility.
