9-
10-## Session Manager owner boundary
11-
12-`task-board codex` and `task-board claude` are thin clients of the board-keyed
13-Task-board Session Manager. `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic and renders
14-the non-launching primary-session plan. The manager owns provider lifetime and
15-native reflection for both parent and spawned owners.
16-
17-| Owner | Ledger slot | Provider session |
18-| --- | --- | --- |
19-| Primary wrapper | `parent/primary` | Manager-held Codex app-server/thread or Claude PTY |
20-| Tracked spawn | `run/RUN-*` | Distinct manager-held provider host for that run |
21-
22-The run receives no manager endpoint, provider URL, thread/session identity, or
23-credential. Attach reconciles the exact v2 owner head before the assignment
24-turn. Provider parity covers requirement delivery, directives, recovery,
25-handoff evidence, acceptance, and audit; provider mechanics remain private to
26-the Codex and Claude host adapters.
27-
28----
29-
30-## What Is Tracked Background Spawn
31-
32-Tracked background spawn starts a real agent runtime without blocking the
33-parent/orchestrator shell. Ordinary prompt-mode runs use `claude -p`, `codex
34:exec`, or Qwen stream-JSON; goal-bound Codex/Claude owners execute inside a
35-manager-held session. Every run gets a durable `RUN-...` handle, persisted
36-state, replayable events, watcher output, a wait barrier, directives, logs, and
37-completion hooks.
38-
39-The parent agent is the active Orchestrator role (`orchestrator` / legacy `coordinator`). It creates or selects board tasks, spawns child runs, watches their progress, sends directives when needed, and reads child outcome resources for routing. It should not turn into the worker or reviewer just because a task is small.
40-
41-**Use cases:**
42-- Delegate implementation to a separate agent process with its own context window
43-- Run agents on different model families (Claude vs OpenAI) for diversity
44-- Scale beyond runtime-native helper slots while preserving board tracking
45-- Preserve nested spawn capability across parent/child agent processes
46-
47----
48-
49-## Spawning via `task-board spawn` (Recommended)
50-
51-The `spawn` command is the default way to launch tracked background runs. It handles assignment, prompt composition, persistence, logging, events, and completion hooks automatically.
52-
53-**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen additionally require a model-compatible `--reasoning-effort`; Claude does not accept reasoning. The role drives agent identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. Role resolution precedes ceiling lookup and board access: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, the legacy `coordinator` spelling shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown names fail closed with typed errors.
54-
55-Before spawning, resolve only the target policy:
56-
57-```bash
58-task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'
59-```
60-
61-The projection returns the effective provider policy/schedule, target role
62-ceiling, selection mode, composition status, and local queue capacity without
63-auth/session/version-control data. Query `models(agent=<selected-agent>)` only
64-when the registered set is needed to resolve a model. Full `project_config()`
65-is the audit/debug surface.
66-
67-## Provider System And UTC Schedule
68-
69-`spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is the canonical provider policy. It is an
70-enforced tagged union: `exclusive` contains one provider, while `mixed`
71-contains at least two unique normalized providers. Explicit `--agent` wins over
72-affinity only inside that set. Otherwise exclusive pins its provider and mixed
73-keeps the current Codex/Claude runtime affinity only when the set allows it.
74-Out-of-set explicit providers or affinities are refused before task lookup,
75-assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch.
76-
77-```json
78-{
79-  "spawn": {
80-    "preferred_agentic_system": {
81-      "mixed": ["claude", "codex"]
82-    },
83-    "schedule": {
84-      "windows": [
85-        {
86-          "start_utc": "17:00",
87-          "end_utc": "03:00",
88-          "preferred_agentic_system": {
89-            "exclusive": "qwen"
90-          }
91-        }
92-      ]
93-    }
94-  }
95-}
96-```
97-
98-Schedule bounds are UTC `HH:MM`, start-inclusive/end-exclusive, and disjoint.
99-Later-to-earlier bounds cross midnight; equal or overlapping bounds are
100-invalid. An active window replaces the base provider system before provider
101-and registered-model validation. Outside every window, the base system applies;
102-if no base exists, ordinary runtime affinity remains unrestricted. The selected
103-provider's ceiling is resolved only after this step.
104-
105-`project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is the currently effective
106-`{mode, allowed_providers, source, config_key}` projection.
107-`project_config().spawn.schedule` separately reports `timezone: "UTC"`, all
108-normalized windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system`.
109-Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains readable and projects to an effective
110-exclusive system, but startup diagnoses it with the canonical replacement.
111-The misspelling `preferred_agent_system` is not an alias.
112-
113-## Project MCP Launch Composition
114-
115-Tracked children inherit the project's enabled MCP servers through the versioned `agents-infra.child-launch-composition` contract. This capability is default-on:
116-
117-```json
118-{
119-  "spawn": {
120-    "launch_composition": {
121-      "enabled": true
122-    }
123-  }
124-}
125-```
126-
127-Omitting either `launch_composition` or `enabled` means `true`; explicit `false` is the only opt-out. Local mode reads `spawn.launch_composition.enabled`. Remote mode reads `remote.spawn.launch_composition.enabled`, with the existing whole-policy root fallback only when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. A present remote policy with the field omitted defaults on instead of inheriting the root field. `project_config().spawn.launch_composition` reports the effective boolean, active dotted key, contract, schema version `1`, and `bare_with_evidence` degradation policy without invoking infra.
128-
129-After agent selection and work-directory resolution, each ordinary prompt-mode
130-spawn invokes the non-launching child composition command:
131-
132-```bash
133-agents-infra compose --agent qwen --project "$PWD" --schema-version 1 --json
134-```
135-
136-Task-board validates the contract identity, canonical project, selected provider, required metadata, and the complete provider allowlist before using any prefix. Codex accepts only `-c mcp_servers.<name>.(url|bearer_token_env_var|command|args)=...` pairs. Claude and Qwen accept only one `--mcp-config` JSON value rooted at `mcpServers`, with the contract's HTTP or stdio shapes. Any extra top-level flag, primary-session policy, unknown field, server mismatch, malformed JSON, or literal bearer value rejects the whole prefix; task-board never salvages individual arguments.
137-
138-The accepted prefix is prepended at the provider's top-level boundary for
139:ordinary prompt-mode launches. A goal-bound Codex/Claude owner instead requires
140-`agents-infra compose --mode primary-session`; task-board validates and
141-persists that exact managed-host plan for the Session Manager. Qwen does not
142:support goal-bound owners. Restart, reroute, and autonomous recovery successors
143-retain the plan and rebind only the new `RUN-*` owner context.
144-
145-| Condition | Child launch | Sanitized status |
146-|---|---|---|
147-| Explicit opt-out | Bare | `disabled` |
148-| Valid prefix with MCP servers | Composed | `applied` |
149-| Valid empty MCP set | Bare-equivalent | `empty` |
150-| `agents-infra` missing | Bare | `degraded_tool_missing` |
151-| Old/pre-contract command | Bare | `degraded_contract_unavailable` |
152-| Unsupported contract/schema | Bare | `degraded_unsupported_schema` |
153-| Recognized composition failure | Bare | `degraded_compose_failed` |
154-| Malformed, mismatched, or disallowed payload | Bare | `degraded_invalid_payload` |
155-
156-Degradation is fail-open only for this optional capability: it never weakens or rewrites board-owned safety flags. `spawn --json` returns the safe summary under `launchComposition`; task notes and durable run state/events carry the same snake-case summary. The private manifest alone stores the validated MCP argv snapshot needed by the detached runner. Public evidence never contains argv payloads, URLs, commands, command arguments, headers, external stderr, or environment values.
157-
158-`bearer_token_env_var` is a name reference, not a value lookup. Task-board and agents-infra do not resolve it during composition. The child inherits unrelated caller environment variables through the existing provider filters, while evidence retains only referenced variable names. Do not put token values in task prompts, notes, resources, manifests, events, logs, or command diagnostics.
159-
160-## Board-Managed Selection Procedure
161-
162-Choose every spawn for **maximum task quality per token**, not by reflexively
163-using the strongest model or highest supported effort.
164-
165-1. Assess the task's scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation
166-   burden, and expected context and token cost (prompt, source material,
167-   iterations, and likely retries).
168-2. Query the registry and select the strongest suitable explicit model/effort
169-   pair that can credibly pass the existing quality gates.
170-3. For models that support it, use **`max` as the highest recommended
171-   board-managed effort**. `ultra` remains explicitly compatible, but enables
172-   model-internal delegation rather than deeper reasoning.
173-4. Prefer task-board roles, tracked `RUN-...` lifecycle, dependencies, and
174-   reviewer routing as the predictable delegation plane. That keeps work
175-   observable instead of duplicating delegation inside a model call.
176-5. Use a lower-cost pair only when bounded scope can still meet the same
177-   required test, validation, outcome, and review gates.
178-
179-Choose the role from the deliverable, not from the provider or model:
180-
181-| Task type | Role |
182-| --- | --- |
183-| Research, analysis, or investigation | `researcher` |
184-| Code implementation, bug fixes, or refactoring | `developer` |
185-| Test implementation or validation | `tester` |
186-| Genuine system-design tradeoff or unresolved cross-module contract | `solution-architect` |
187-| Documentation | `doc-writer` |
188-| Code, documentation, or acceptance review | `reviewer` |
189-| Board decomposition, routing, and delivery supervision | `orchestrator` (`coordinator` is the legacy alias) |
190-
191-The current registry remains the executable source of truth. The table below
192-preserves the human selection tiers and model-specific effort caps; query
193-`models(agent=<selected-agent>)` before launch for the live registered set.
194-
195-| Work tier | Agent | Model | Recommended effort | Highest supported effort |
196-| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
197-| Cross-module, novel, risky, or review-critical | Codex | `gpt-5.6-sol` | `max` | `ultra` |
198-| Balanced agentic work | Codex | `gpt-5.6-terra` | `max` | `ultra` |
199-| Fast, affordable agentic work | Codex | `gpt-5.6-luna` | `max` | `max` (no `ultra`) |
200-| Complex coding and research | Codex | `gpt-5.5` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` (no `max`/`ultra`) |
201-| Everyday coding | Codex | `gpt-5.4` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` |
202-| Simpler cost-sensitive work | Codex | `gpt-5.4-mini` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` |
203-| Ultra-fast code iteration | Codex | `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` | `xhigh` | `xhigh` |
204-| Hardest, longest-running Claude work | Claude | `claude-fable-5` | none | none; Claude rejects reasoning effort |
205-| Everyday complex Claude work (recommended) | Claude | `claude-opus-5` | none | none; Claude rejects reasoning effort |
206-| Previous-generation everyday Claude work | Claude | `claude-opus-4-8` | none | none; Claude rejects reasoning effort |
207-| Routine Claude work | Claude | `claude-sonnet-5` | none | none; Claude rejects reasoning effort |
208-| Fast, bounded Claude checks | Claude | `claude-haiku-4-5` | none | none; Claude rejects reasoning effort |
209-| Balanced Qwen Token Plan work | Qwen | `qwen3.7-plus` | `xhigh` | `max` |
210-| Highest-capability Qwen preview work | Qwen | `qwen3.8-max-preview` | `xhigh` | `max` |
211-
212-**Budget is unlimited by default.** Do not pass `--budget` unless the user
213-explicitly asks to cap spending. A cap that stops a child halfway usually costs
214-more after the retry; unlimited is cheaper than retries.
215-
216-For prompt-mode Claude spawns, `--budget` maps to Claude's provider-enforced
217:print-mode cap. A manager-owned goal-bound Claude session uses a persistent PTY,
218-where that provider flag is invalid; the requested amount is retained in run
219-audit state but is advisory and not a hard cap. Do not promise a hard USD limit
220-for that path until manager-side accounting exists.
221-
222-For example, `ultra` remains a valid explicit compatibility selection when a
