380-
381-## Wait And Completion Mechanics
382-
383-`task-board spawn wait RUN-...` does **not** wait on a shell background job and does **not** try to keep track of the child agent PID from the original `task-board spawn` caller.
384-
385-This section summarizes the runtime behavior. The durable source of truth for completion semantics is [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](../.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).
386-
387-Instead, the contract is:
388-
389-1. `task-board spawn` writes `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../{manifest.json,state.json}`
390-2. Background `spawn-runner` process starts and moves the run from `queued` to `running`
391-3. That runner launches the real child agent and blocks on it directly
392-4. When the child agent exits, the runner executes the completion hook
393-5. The completion hook syncs the spawn-log resource, appends completion notes, and applies any post-run artifact/status checks
394-6. Only after that does the runner write terminal run state (`completed` or `failed`) back to `state.json`
395-7. `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` polls `state.json` until it sees a terminal status
396-
397-This is why coordinators should treat `RUN-...` as the handle for orchestration barriers. The shell process that originally invoked `task-board spawn` is intentionally not the source of truth once the tracked background runner has been registered.
398-
399-The runner also records its PID and updates a heartbeat while it is alive, including while queued or paused. Wait/follow observers run PID-aware stale repair: a live PID survives delayed heartbeats, while dead `running`, `queued`, or `paused` runners become failed instead of hanging observers forever.
400-
401-### Fused Spawn And Wait (`spawn ... --wait`)
402-
403-`--wait` fuses the two-step `spawn` + `spawn wait` flow into one CLI invocation. The two-step flow remains fully supported; fused `--wait` is a convenience for the single-child / single-barrier case, not a replacement.
404-
405-```bash
406-task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --wait
407-```
408-
409-Contract:
410-
411-- `--background` is still required. `--wait` changes observation, not execution placement: the child agent still runs under the tracked background runner, and the run is registered identically to the two-step flow — same `RUN-...` handle, manifest/state/events under `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`, directive plane, completion hook, and observation hints.
412-- After queueing, the same in-process wait loop as standalone `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` takes over (they share one code path, so polling, terminal reporting, and exit semantics cannot drift). The command blocks until the run reaches a terminal state.
413-- Exit code matches standalone `spawn wait`: success only when the run reaches `completed`; `failed`, `cancelled`, and any other non-completed terminal state return a non-zero error exit.
414-- The fused wait has no wait-side timeout; it blocks until terminal state. `spawn --timeout` remains the child agent process timeout. Use standalone `spawn wait RUN-... --timeout 5m` when you need a bounded barrier.
415-- Text output prints the queued `RUN-...` and observation commands first (marked "Fused wait active: no separate spawn wait invocation is needed"), then the terminal report. In `--json` mode the command waits first and emits a single response whose `runStatus`/`exitCode` reflect the terminal state, with the usual `observation.*` contract included.
416-
417-Claude-Code orchestrator example — ONE Bash tool call, wrapped by the caller in `run_in_background: true`:
418-
419-```bash
420-# One harness Bash call (run_in_background: true) — registers the tracked run,
421-# blocks in-process to terminal state, and its exit wakes the Claude session:
422-task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5 --wait
423-```
424-
425-**Hard boundary — `run_in_background` stays caller/harness-side.** Whether that single `spawn ... --wait` invocation blocks the orchestrator session or runs as a harness background task is decided by the caller (the Claude Code `Bash` tool's `run_in_background` parameter), and only by the caller. There is no API through which the CLI or server could place itself into a harness background slot, so task-board cannot emulate or move that decision CLI/server-side. This is a structural boundary of the harness model, not a bug or missing feature. The fused flag only reduces two CLI calls to one; harness placement remains the orchestrator's decision at every callsite.
426-
427-When to prefer which flow:
428-
429-- **Fused `--wait`**: exactly one child and one barrier — a single sequenced producer step, or a Claude orchestrator that wants one background Bash call per child instead of two.
430-- **Two-step `spawn` + `spawn wait`**: fan-out of several independent children first, then one background wait per `RUN-...`; bounded waits (`--timeout`); or waiting from a different process/session than the one that spawned.
431-
