433-
434-## Orchestrator Monitoring Patterns
435-
436-`task-board spawn` is background execution with tracking. Do not wrap it in shell backgrounding (`&`, `nohup`, runtime `run_in_background`) just to avoid blocking; the command already returns once the tracked runner is registered.
437-
438-### Pattern A: Fan Out Tracked Background Runs
439-
440-```bash
441-task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
442-task-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
443-task-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role tester --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
444-```
445-
446-Each call returns a `RUN-...` identifier plus a task- and run-scoped logwork path. Concurrent runs for the same task and role therefore keep separate log files and outcome resources. The runner enforces shared concurrency from `task-board.config.json -> spawn.max_parallel` (default `20`), so you can queue more runs than the machine is allowed to execute at once.
447-
448-### Pattern B: Replay And Follow Event Cursors
449-
450-```bash
451-task-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123
452-task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123
453-task-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
454-task-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5
455-task-board spawn observe RUN-260320-abc123 --cursor RUN-260320-abc123:5 --until terminal --format compact
456-task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
457-task-board spawn directives RUN-260320-abc123
458-task-board spawn directive RUN-260320-abc123 cancel
459:task-board spawn goal RUN-260320-abc123
460-```
461-
462-Every valid explicit spawn prints these monitor commands automatically after queueing, seeded with cursor `RUN-ID:0` and bound to the same `task-board` executable / board context that launched the run.
463-In JSON mode the same contract is returned under `observation`:
464-`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, and `inSessionDelivery`.
465-`observation.runtime` reports the orchestrator session runtime (`codex-session`, `claude-session`, or `external`) and `observation.delivery` names its non-blocking completion channel (`codex-in-session`, `claude-background-wait`, or `external-watch`).
466-
467-Use the printed `observe --until terminal --format compact` command as the
468-default single surface: it consumes events newer than its durable cursor and
469-appends terminal checklist/outcome handoff evidence without replaying earlier
470-history. Use `status` for a diagnostic snapshot, `events` for replay, `watch`
471-for follow mode, and `wait` only for a barrier that does not need event/handoff
472-detail. Do not stack these reads on the routine success path.
473-In JSON mode, `spawn watch` uses notification NDJSON framing only (one notification object per line, no trailing summary payload).
474-
475-### Pattern C: Durable Directive Plane
476-
477-- `spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` records a durable operator directive with acknowledgement state.
478-- `spawn directives RUN-...` replays the directive history for that run.
479-- `cancel` is applied immediately for queued runs and terminates the background runner/process group for running runs when the platform supports it.
480-- `pause_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, and `nudge` are cooperative signals. Current runtimes expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`; the child may poll `task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"` at safe checkpoints.
481-- `restart` and `reroute_after_checkpoint` create successor `RUN-...` records instead of rewriting existing run history. Running reroute/pause requests are rejected when the runtime cannot checkpoint safely in place.
482-- Both directive commands print the current active goal header before directive details. New records persist the immutable goal ID/revision under which they were issued; legacy records print `issued_under_goal: unknown`.
483:- Restart and a reroute that preserves the current goal ID, revision, role binding, scope, kind, predicate, and intent remain owner-agent recovery actions. Goal-bound `cancel` and non-monotonic reroute are operator-authoritative controls and require a non-empty reason. A structured replacement validates its requested scope/kind/predicate against the successor role before it writes the directive or successor.
484-
485-### Pattern D: Goal-Bound Delivery Pool
486-
487-After plan approval and before the first producer launch or delivery status transition, start one independently tracked orchestrator owner:
488-
489-```bash
490-task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth \
491-  --role orchestrator \
492-  --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b \
493-  --background \
494-  --agent codex \
495-  --model gpt-5.6-sol \
496-  --reasoning-effort max
497-```
498-
499-The CLI resolves containers to an active dependency-closed task/bug pool,
500-derives `delivery_pool/accepted_done` from the normalized orchestrator role,
501-reserves the RUN/GOAL lineage, validates provider capability and the
502-primary-session launch plan, and only then permits normal launch side effects.
503-The Session Manager attaches the exact `run/RUN-*` owner, reconciles its native
504-goal, and submits the assignment only after the session's applied revision
505-matches the authoritative v2 head. Nested specialists receive role-derived
506:`role_handoff` or `reviewer_verdict` owners without replacing the parent
507-`delivery_pool` condition.
508-
509-The authoritative state is the `board-goal-v2` `run/RUN-*` owner head and its
510-immutable revision history. `manifest.launch_goal`, `state.active_goal_ref`,
511-and run-local revision files are one-release `board-goal-v1` compatibility
512-mirrors written with the v2 promotion. Failed native activation leaves the
513-prior head active; an unbound head is `pending-unbound` until the provider
514-acknowledges that exact revision.
515-
516-Parent and run owner heads are isolated manager slots. The spawned provider
517-environment strips parent manager/app-server endpoints, auth indirections and
518-their token values, and parent provider session markers. Persisted caller-plane
519-fields are decode-only compatibility data; new launches issue no caller RPC.
520-One-release restoration remains only for an active legacy manifest that
521-already contains an exact caller snapshot.
522-
523-At every checkpoint:
524-
525-```bash
526:task-board spawn goal "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
527-task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
528-```
529-
530-If a nested target resolves outside the current pool, `spawn` returns non-zero `goal_scope_mismatch` before assignment, prompt, manifest, provider, or child-process effects. The payload classifies missing leaves as `adjacent`, `outside`, or `mixed` and includes the exact optimistic update command. Run that command only when the work is genuinely required, then retry the spawn.
531-
532-Nested spawn also fails closed on its declared parent execution context. While
533-a legacy already-running Claude owner has `successor_required`, the command
534-returns typed `goal_provider_binding_pending` with `side_effects=false`; new
535:goal-bound Claude owners start directly in manager-held PTYs. If the declared
536-parent manifest, state, or active revision cannot be loaded, the command
537-returns `goal_parent_context_unavailable` rather than treating the caller as
538-non-goal.
539-
540-Owner-agent changes are monotonic: create with `--scope`, expand with `--add-scope`, refresh only the non-authoritative intent, and supply `--if-revision` for updates. Upsert responses expose `mutation.previous_revision` as `0` on creation, the prior active revision on a new immutable revision, and the current revision on an idempotent replay. Restart and same-semantics reroute retain that latest revision under a rebound successor owner. Scope removal, structural replacement, weakening, kind changes, and cancellation cross the operator directive boundary and create durable cancelled/superseded lineage rather than successful delivery.
541-
542-For a legacy already-running Claude run, `successor_required` retains its
543-one-release safe-checkpoint behavior. New manager-owned sessions do not use
544-that split path.
545-
546-Provider usage/budget limits, transient launch or transport failures, nonzero goal-owner exits, unmet producer handoff status/evidence, missing reviewer branches, and unmet delivery acceptance persist a structured recovery cause and automatically queue a successor with the same active goal revision. The predecessor is superseded by recovery, never successful delivery.
547-
548-If successor log preparation or runner startup fails, its manifest and state
549-remain non-terminal `queued` with a persisted deferred-start cause, retry time,
550-and scheduler lease. Status, wait, and follow observers claim an eligible or
551-expired lease under the scheduler lock, retry that same successor outside the
552-lock, and clear the deferred marker only after runner start/heartbeat. The
553-predecessor may already be superseded, but the same immutable goal revision
554-still has exactly one retryable owner instead of two terminal runs.
555-
556-Provider `blocked` and a role that exits at board `blocked` use the same
557-Stop-The-Line gate. Board `blocked` plus a new or updated task-scoped evidence
558-packet terminates the run with `completion_reason=stop_boundary`; reviewer runs
559-also persist the `stop_the_line` verdict. This branch never satisfies delivery
560-acceptance. Without the status/evidence pair, the boundary claim is recoverable
561-and the same revision is queued again.
562-
563-Producer provider exit is not handoff success. A `role_handoff` run must reach its immutable role end status and add or update a task-scoped outcome artifact relative to its launch snapshot. Zero exit while the board remains in `analysis`/`development`, or at the handoff status without evidence, queues autonomous same-revision recovery.
564-
565-After attaching the task-scoped outcome, producers should use
566-`task-board handoff TASK-ID --role ROLE`. The command resolves the configured
567-role end status, verifies every checklist item and an actual stored task-scoped
568-outcome, then performs the evidence decision and ordinary status transition
569-under one mutation lock. It reports all missing checklist/outcome evidence and
570-leaves status unchanged when the gate fails. Spawn-log artifacts do not count as
571-producer outcomes, and the runtime still enforces its launch-relative outcome
572-snapshot independently.
573-
574-Delivery success is not provider completion alone. Reviewer goals persist exactly one evidence-backed branch derived from board state: `accepted`, `changes_requested`, or `stop_the_line`; an exit while still `reviewing` triggers recovery. `required`/`light` leaves need board `done`, task-scoped producer outcome evidence, and a later reviewer whose persisted branch is `accepted`. `none` leaves need `done`, producer outcome evidence, and the owner’s completion-time evidence check.
575-
576-### Pattern E: Delivery Boundary (Watcher vs In-Session)
577-
578-- `spawn events`, `spawn watch`, and `spawn observe` are external watcher surfaces built on persisted run events.
579-- They can notify a human/operator and feed TUI/remote push adapters.
580-- They do **not** inject inbound text into the currently active orchestrator session by default.
581-- When the orchestrator session is launched through `task-board codex`, tracked terminal state is also injected back into the active Codex thread through the app-server runtime adapter.
582-- `task-board codex` may also auto-steer safe `waitingOnUserInput` pauses inside that active thread when `session_manager.providers.codex.auto_continue.enabled=true` or `--auto-continue` is set for the launch.
583-- The v1 anti-stall controller never auto-continues `waitingOnApproval` and does not try to answer child-process terminal prompts on behalf of the operator.
584-- If runtime-native direct push is unavailable, use terminal delta observation (`spawn observe --cursor ... --until terminal --format compact`) as the default; replay (`spawn events --cursor ...`) and snapshot checks (`spawn status`) remain diagnostic alternatives.
585-
586-These are observation/session enhancements only. They do not determine whether
587:either primary launcher can create a goal-bound tracked owner.
588-
589-### Pattern F: Claude Orchestrator Non-Blocking Fan-Out
590-
591-Claude Code has no injection API for a live session, so there is no Claude
592-counterpart of the Codex app-server push adapter. The equivalent non-blocking
593-channel is the harness itself: Claude Code re-invokes the session when a
594-tracked background command exits. Spawn detects a Claude session through the
595-`CLAUDECODE` environment variable and reports
596-`observation.delivery = claude-background-wait`.
597-
598-The orchestrator pattern:
599-
600-1. Spawn all independent children first — each fully selected tracked spawn returns its `RUN-...` immediately.
601-2. For every returned run, start the printed `task-board spawn wait RUN-...` command as a **harness background task** (Claude Code `Bash` with `run_in_background`), not as a blocking foreground call.
602-3. Keep orchestrating: the session stays free while children execute; each background wait exits when its run reaches terminal state, and the harness wakes the session with that result.
603-4. On each wake-up, inspect `spawn status RUN-...` / outcome resources and route the next step (reviewer spawn, rework, dependent task) — then return to waiting on the remaining runs.
604-
605-```bash
606-# One Claude orchestrator, five concurrent children, zero blocking:
607-task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5   # -> RUN-A
608-task-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-5   # -> RUN-B
609-# ... spawn remaining children ...
610-# then one harness background task per run:
611-#   run_in_background: task-board spawn wait RUN-A
612-#   run_in_background: task-board spawn wait RUN-B
613-```
614-
615-Do not wrap a plain `task-board spawn` itself in a background helper — `--background`
616-already returns a tracked run. Only the `spawn wait` observation barrier goes
617-into the harness background slot. Sequenced work (producer -> reviewer) still
618-uses an explicit `spawn wait` barrier before spawning the dependent child.
619-
620-Single-child variant: the fused `task-board spawn TASK ... --wait` collapses
621-steps 1–2 into ONE harness background Bash call per child — it registers the
622-tracked run identically and then blocks in-process to terminal state, so its
623-exit wakes the session exactly like a standalone background `spawn wait` (see
624-"Fused Spawn And Wait" above). Wrapping that single call in `run_in_background`
625-remains the caller/harness decision — task-board cannot make it.
626-
627-### Removed: Launch-Time Foreground Mode
628-
629-The old launch-time foreground/attached harness (the original foreground `task-board spawn TASK --wait`, hidden `--attached`, and proposed `--foreground`) has been removed from the public spawn path. It mixed execution placement with observation and made the parent/orchestrator act like a worker.
630-
631-The current `--wait` flag is not that mode returning: it is a fused observation barrier on top of the unchanged tracked background runner (see "Fused Spawn And Wait" above). Execution placement stays background-only — `--background` is required with or without `--wait`.
632-
633-Use tracked background spawn first, then wait on the `RUN-...` handle when a blocking barrier is needed:
634-
635-```bash
636-task-board spawn TASK-260214-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max
637-task-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123
638-```
639-
640-The postmortem for that removal is attached to `TASK-260619-spid7i` as `TASK-260619-spid7i_foreground-harness-postmortem.md`.
