50-## 2. Installation lifecycle
51-
52-The manager MUST implement installation as read-only planning, optional private
53-compilation, and one serialized publication transaction. This lifecycle applies
54-to project, global, and hybrid installation, upgrade, and repair. Package code
55-MUST NOT execute during any phase; the closed `go-v1` compiler process graph
56-below is the only exception to passing package bytes to a trusted tool.
57-
58:### 2.1 Read-only planning and source gates
59-
60-For each operation the manager performs these phases in order:
61-
62-1. load `Skillfile.json`, or skip an absent manifest, and determine effective
63-   agents and locale;
64-2. verify generated project and adapter paths are ignored by git, load
65-   development substitutions, reject them under strict audit, and add
66-   applicable hybrid declarations;
67-3. resolve every ref to an immutable raw snapshot and validate its complete
68-   tree before reading a build-cache entry or starting any Go process;
69-4. parse and validate both manifest names according to Protocol Core section
70-   4, validate link-free disjoint runtime roots, build roots, and source
71-   directories, and derive every artifact-relative path from its command name;
72-5. exclude every build root as a whole subtree from agent context before
73-   locale rendering and from commit-keyed runtime copying, identically for a
74-   real build, cache hit, and dry run;
75-6. compute `curator-build-source-v1` over every regular file in each fully
76-   validated raw snapshot, including a package-provided root
77-   `.csk-install.json`, and freeze that snapshot instance until its last build
78-   child exits;
79-7. build the provider-first dependency closure, apply source allowlists and
80-   snapshot checks, validate every skill package, reject command, shim,
81-   portable-path, case-folding, and platform-path collisions, and verify system,
82-   legacy command, and MCP requirements;
83-8. run source-audit policy, resolve trusted registries and attestations, reject
84-   revocation or strict unknown results, and apply moved-tag policy; and
85-9. only after all preceding gates pass, resolve and fingerprint a trusted
86-   toolchain, derive build inputs and logical cache keys, validate the protected
87-   cache boundary, and inspect eligible receipts and artifacts read-only.
88-
89-Snapshot validation MUST reject links, special files, invalid protocol paths,
90-duplicate encoded paths, and platform path collisions. A build root MUST be a
91-real link-free directory below the snapshot, MUST NOT be `.`, MUST be disjoint
92-from every runtime root, and MUST contain the nearest ancestor `go.mod` of each
93-of its commands' `source_dir` values. Every declared build root MUST be used.
94-Generated artifacts remain only in manager-owned staging and immutable cache
95-state and MUST NOT become agent-facing context.
96-
97-Audit and registry gates therefore precede both persistent cache lookup and
98-compilation. Neither a cache hit nor a dry run may bypass source validation,
99-static context exclusion, build-source hashing, closure construction, collision
100-checks, audit, attestation, revocation, or moved-tag evaluation.
101-
102-### 2.2 Closed `go-v1` toolchain and process graph
103-
104-A conforming manager MUST support an operator-trusted Go 1.23 release family
105-that it has tested against the `go-v1` conformance vectors. It MAY allowlist
106-additional Go release families only after testing those families against the
107-same vectors. It MUST reject a pre-1.23, malformed, package-selected, or
108-otherwise unknown release, including a newer release merely ordered after a
109-known one. The launcher is selected independently of the package and MUST be
110-the regular executable `<GOROOT>/bin/go` (`bin/go.exe` on Windows), never a
111-wrapper, repository file, runtime command, project shim, manifest value, or
112-executable found through the user `PATH`.
113-
114-Before entering a package-controlled directory, the manager creates an
115-operation-private probe root. From a manager-owned empty working directory it
116-invokes the resolved `go` executable directly with exactly these first three
117-argument vectors, once per operation:
118-
119-```text
120-[<resolved-go>, "telemetry", "off"]
121-[<resolved-go>, "version"]
122-[<resolved-go>, "env", "-json", "GOROOT", "GOHOSTOS", "GOHOSTARCH", "GOOS", "GOARCH", "GO386", "GOAMD64", "GOARM", "GOARM64", "GOMIPS", "GOMIPS64", "GOPPC64", "GORISCV64", "GOWASM", "GOTELEMETRY", "GOTELEMETRYDIR"]
123-```
124-
125-The manager requires the probed `GOROOT` to equal the independently resolved
126-root, normalizes the bounded single-line `go version` output, verifies the
127-native host and target values, and only then fingerprints the selected tree
128-using `curator-go-toolchain-v1` as defined by Protocol Core section 8.2.

