## Blocking findings

1. The next wire shapes contradict the shared toolchain contract. Decision 0008 lines 194-202 says a schema-8 local command contains exactly `type`, `driver`, and `source_dir`. Lines 230-241 say descriptor schema 2 changes only the driver union, adds no member, and forbids a toolchain version. The current decision 0007 lines 343-346 requires a REQUIRED `toolchain` object in the next manifest schema and an OPTIONAL descriptor-target requirement in the next `skill-build.json` schema, with this task owning their version numbers. Decision 0008 references that contract but leaves no legal location for either object. This makes downstream TASK-260728-2jaw7h/TASK-260728-251p01 impossible without violating one accepted decision. Rework must synchronize both decisions and fix the exact closed local and descriptor target shapes while retaining the ban on paths, URLs, channels, install commands, arbitrary argv/environment, and trust roots.

2. `manager-worker-v1` is semantically widened while claimed unchanged. Decision 0006 lines 39-58 fixes the identity to the Go-specific graph and exactly one `go list` plus one `go build`; its lines 131-133 state that a process-graph semantic change requires cache-identity separation. Decision 0008 lines 293-326 instead gives the same identity a driver-generic launcher/toolchain graph and driver-defined graph/compile commands. The assertion at lines 311-316 that the lower nodes were always per-operation values is not supported by the frozen definition. The rejected-alternative claim at lines 503-506 is also false as stated: a new policy identity for only new drivers need not change any Go cache key or rc.5 byte. Rework must either preserve Go on `manager-worker-v1` and version the new-driver policy, or provide a versioned composition model that proves the old policy meaning and Go semantics are not reinterpreted.

3. Admission language is internally contradictory. Decision 0008 lines 86-99 says Protocol 1.0 admits all eight listed values, while lines 108-112 says six are reservations and not wire admission. The validator and task handoff treat only the two Go identifiers as admitted. Replace this with separate closed reserved-namespace and currently admitted-wire sets so implementers and claim readers have one normative answer.

4. The deterministic boundary gate does not enforce the boundary it claims. `tools/validate.py` lines 388-396 and 484-514 inspect only `common.schema.json` definitions that already have a `driver`, validate only that property, and reject only a short artifact-member name list. A read-only monkey-patch probe added optional `language` to `buildCommandV6`, arbitrary `command` to `skillBuildTargetV1`, and `runtime_files` to `buildRecordV2`; `validate_additional_driver_boundary()` accepted all three. Add semantic negative coverage for the exact local/descriptor member sets, forbidden generic selectors and command/install fields, and the single-artifact model, including the new schema versions once admitted.

